{
  "image_name": "d7f74d9e02361d0852680858011d4c5f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how two reductive enzymes, azoreductase (AZR) and nitroreductase (NTR), are used to activate therapeutic or responsive materials. At the top, it shows the core chemistry: AZR cleaves azo bonds into amines, while NTR reduces nitro groups to amines. The rest of the figure applies this idea across several nanomedicine designs.\n\nA recurring theme is that reduction changes molecular structure, which then disrupts assemblies or switches function. In azocalixarene systems and other azo-containing aggregates, AZR cleavage appears to break amphiphilic or host-like molecules apart, causing release of encapsulated cargo such as curcumin. In nitro-containing cationic oligomers complexed with siRNA, NTR reduction seems to convert cationic binding units into less interactive products, leading to disassembly and nucleic acid release. A similar trigger is shown for nitro-containing liposomes, where membrane components are reduced and the vesicle leaks its payload.\n\nThe lower part highlights therapeutic activation. A PDI-based drug undergoes bioreduction, altering its conjugated core, while a metal-containing polymer with nitroaromatic ligands is linked to Fe(III)/Fe(II) chemistry and glutathione/hydrogen peroxide, suggesting redox-responsive behavior for combined chemodynamic and nitric oxide therapy. Overall, the figure emphasizes enzyme-triggered bond reduction as a general strategy for selective drug release and treatment activation.",
